{
  "title": "Multi-recipe pages get merged into one record with instructions \
from only one recipe",
  "description": "When a source page contains two distinct recipes under \
separate headings (title + attribution + ingredient list + method), the \
pipeline collapses them into a single record and keeps instructions from only \
one of them. Ingredients from both recipes end up flattened into one list with \
`section: null`, so shared item names (salt, lemon juice, pepper) appear twice \
with no indication of which recipe they belong to.\n\
\n\
Concrete example: \
https://smittenkitchen.com/2009/09/grilled-lamb-kebabs-tzatziki/ (cached at \
~/.ramekin/http-cache/smittenkitchen-com_2009-09-grilled-lamb-kebabs-tzatziki/). \
The source has a `<b>Yogurt-Marinated Lamb Kebabs</b>` section followed by a \
`<b>Tzatziki</b>` section, each with its own ingredient block and method. The \
snapshot at \
data/pipeline-snapshots/smittenkitchen-com_2009-09-grilled-lamb-kebabs-tzatziki.json \
is titled 'Yogurt-Marinated Lamb Kebabs' but its `instructions` field contains \
only the tzatziki procedure ('Place the yogurt in a medium bowl. Grate the \
cucumber...'). The entire lamb procedure -- combining the marinade, cubing the \
lamb, grilling the skewers with onion -- is dropped. All 18 ingredients from \
both recipes are concatenated with no sectioning.\n\
\n\
Ideal fix is to emit one recipe per heading block. Acceptable fallback: keep \
one record but preserve every method paragraph and populate `section` on \
ingredients so the two sub-recipes are distinguishable.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "pipeline",
    "extraction",
  ],
